rimental)\n\n> Abstract:We address an old open question in convex geometry that dates back to the work of Minkowski: what are the equality cases of the monotonicity of mixed volumes? The problem is equivalent to that of providing a geometric characterization of the support of mixed area measures. A conjectural characterization was put forward by Schneider (1985), but has been verified to date only for special classes of convex bodies. In this paper we resolve one direction of Schneider's conjecture for arbitrary convex bodies in R n$\\mathbb{R}^n$, and resolve the full conjecture in R 3$\\mathbb{R}^3$. Among the implications of these results is a mixed counterpart of the classical fact, due to Monge, Hartman--Nirenberg, and Pogorelov, that a surface with vanishing Gaussian curvature is a ruled surface.\nCite as:  | arXiv:2507.20082 [math.MG]\n   | (or arXiv:2507.20082v1 [math.MG] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2507.20082 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Shouda Wang [view email]\n[v1] Sat, 26 Jul 2025 23:38:49 UTC (34 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On Minkowski's monotonicity problem, by Ramon van Handel and Shouda Wang\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMINIMALITY VIA SECOND VARIATION (https://cvgmt.sns.it/media/doc/paper/540/Acerbi-Fusco-Morini.FINAL.pdf)\nciteturn4search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; E.ACERBI, N.FUSCO, M.MORINI ... a quantitative estimate of the deviation from minimality for sets close to E in L1. ... [20], who proved that the strict positivity of the second variation implies local minimality with re-\n4\nE.ACERBI, N.FUSCO, M.MORINI\nWe also mention, as a simple consequence of Theorem 1.1, that in any dimension and for any\nγ > 0 lamellar conﬁgurations are local minimizers, provided that the number of strips is suﬃciently\nlarge (s